-- title:
--   Factoring a compatible pair through a compositum
-- statement:
--   Let $k$, $L$, $F$, $E$ be fields with $L$ and $F$ algebras over $k$, and let $\alpha_0,\alpha_1 : L \to F$ be two $k$-algebra homomorphisms. Assume (i) the subfield of $F$ generated by the union of the images of $\alpha_0$ and $\alpha_1$ is all of $F$, i.e. $\mathrm{Subfield.closure}(\operatorname{range}\alpha_0 \cup \operatorname{range}\alpha_1) = \top$, and (ii) the underlying ring homomorphism $\alpha_0 : L \to F$ is integral, that is, every element of $F$ is a root of a monic polynomial with coefficients in $\alpha_0(L)$. Let $\beta_0,\beta_1 : L \to E$ be ring homomorphisms (no compatibility with $k$ is assumed) satisfying the relation-transfer hypothesis: for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and all families $x,y : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to L$, if $\sum_i \alpha_0(x_i)\,\alpha_1(y_i) = 0$ in $F$ then $\sum_i \beta_0(x_i)\,\beta_1(y_i) = 0$ in $E$. Then there is a ring homomorphism $\iota : F \to E$ with $\iota \circ \alpha_0 = \beta_0$ and $\iota \circ \alpha_1 = \beta_1$.
--
--   This is the statement that a pair of homomorphisms out of $L$ which respects all additive–multiplicative relations between the two given embeddings of $L$ into $F$ descends to the compositum $F$ of the two images. It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfel'd part of the development, to produce a homomorphism of function fields from germs compatible with the two degeneracy maps.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgHom_exists_ringHom_comp_eq_of_closure_range_union_eq_top_of_forall_sum_mul_eq_zero.lean

